const CARRY = {"fixG":{"phase":"G-classroom: whyProbe playback, the prep lock, the report route — reviewer fixes","applied":["BLOCKER (judge.ts:159, both reviewers). readLocally ignored `probe.askedAs` and accepted matchSpokenAnswer's two ORDINAL readings — `letter` and `position` — which both return confidence 1 and are therefore unescalatable. REPRODUCED BEFORE FIXING, with a real failing test: a four-option PROSE whyProbe on \"How many chambers does the human heart have?\", correct = index 0, student says \"four\" → resolved LOCALLY as WRONG (match.ts's numericOptions guard at :154 does not fire because no option is a bare digit). class-prep/src/prompts.ts:377 instructs the planner to emit `\"askedAs\":\"open\"` for every normal ask, so this was the default case, not an edge. FIX: `const sawAList = probe.askedAs === 'options' && (ask.options?.length ?? 0) > 0;` and `if (!sawAList && (match.by === 'letter' || match.by === 'position')) return null;` — null is the escalation. BOTH conditions are required: a probe claiming 'options' over an ask that shows none is still a student looking at nothing. The `words` and `dont-know` readings survive; they have a referent in what was said rather than in an order never seen.","BLOCKER (whyProbePlayback.test.ts:71, both reviewers). The one test covering the letter path asserted the defect as correct behaviour on an `askedAs: 'open'` fixture — which is why the hole survived. SPLIT: the 'B hoga na' test now uses `askedAs: 'options'` WITH a mirroring `ask.options` (the confusable-pairs exception, where a list genuinely is on screen) and still resolves locally; a new mirror case asserts the same utterance ESCALATES on an open ask. Added the killer case from the finding — a correct \"four\" against a four-option open prose probe must reach the model — plus \"doosra\" escalating, and a verbatim words match still resolving locally so the fix is not over-broad.","BLOCKER (judge.ts:168, adversarial reviewer). A CORRECT answer ending in a Hinglish hedge was filed WRONG locally with no model in the loop. matchSpokenAnswer matches DONT_KNOW by `startsWith(phrase+' ')` / `endsWith(' '+phrase)` (match.ts:128-132), not whole-utterance, and returns confidence 1; readLocally trusted it and returned the ask's hint before `match.index === null` could escalate. REPRODUCED: 'the water rises because the gaps between the particles fill up, pata nahi' and '... i am not sure' both resolved local-wrong. FIX: the local dont-know arm now requires the utterance be at most DONT_KNOW_WORDS (4) words; longer means a student who ANSWERED and then hedged, and only a model can tell that from a shrug. Bare 'pata nahi' is unchanged and its existing test still passes. This was a regression introduced by the wiring — before it, that utterance reached the judge.","SHOULD-FIX (LiveClass.tsx:431, both reviewers). Unguarded `crypto.randomUUID()` on the cold-topic path — a secure-context-only API this repo has already been bitten by and documented at stores/ledger.ts:136 ('http-on-a-LAN is exactly how this gets opened on a school tablet'). The throw landed in the body's `.catch` and painted an error screen, so a non-secure-context device could prepare NO classes. FIX: `mintPrepId()` exported from lib/liveClass.ts (owned), guarded three ways — randomUUID, a try/catch around it for engines that expose-but-refuse, then a hand-rolled v4 off getRandomValues, then Math.random. 4 new tests including 'no crypto at all'. The comment states why this is free: the lock's identity is the bearer token and the relay validates-or-drops x-prep-id, so a fallback id costs attribution and never a lesson. Duplicated from ledger.ts rather than imported — that file is outside this phase's paths and `mint` is module-private there; recorded as a handoff.","SHOULD-FIX (LiveClass.tsx:432-433 and 457-458, adversarial reviewer). A GRANTED lock was leaked for the full 180s TTL when the effect was cleaned up while the take was in flight: `if (cancelled) return;` ran BEFORE `lock = { topicRef, prepId }`, so both the `.finally` and the cleanup found `lock === null` and sent no release — the exact cost the effect's own docblock claims to prevent. FIX: `if (gate.kind === 'granted') lock = { topicRef, prepId };` now runs immediately after each `takePrepLock`, before the cancellation check, at both sites.","SHOULD-FIX (LiveClass.tsx, adversarial reviewer). No unload release existed — grepped and confirmed: the only pagehide/visibilitychange listeners in apps/classroom/src were in classLedger.ts and ledgerSync.ts. A closed tab or a swiped-away app left the lock standing for its whole TTL, which is the commonest way a slow preparation ends. FIX: a `pagehide` listener calling `letGo(true)`, removed in the same cleanup; `releasePrepLock(topicRef, prepId, { unloading })` threads `keepalive: true` into the init `relayFetch` already spreads. DELIBERATE DEVIATION from the reviewer's fix, argued in the comment: NOT on `visibilitychange`. Students background an app constantly and come straight back while the preparation keeps running, so a release on hidden would hand the lock to somebody else mid-spend — it would cause the duplicate the lock exists to stop. `pagehide` needs keepalive and is best-effort by nature; the comment says so rather than claiming a guarantee.","SHOULD-FIX (ReportSheet.tsx:322, adversarial reviewer). The sheet promised a child 'we take those out before it is sent' about their phone number AND address. Verified against packages/safety/src/screen.ts: the ADDRESS regex requires 'flat' / 'house no' / 'my address is' in front of the number, so 'i live at 14 MG Road, Pune' returns verdict 'allow' byte-identical and travels into `student_report.note`. FIX: the copy now promises only what is removed — 'We take out phone numbers and email addresses before it is sent' — and turns the rest into an instruction: 'Please do not write where you live, your school, or anyone's name.' A test asserts the old sentence is gone.","SHOULD-FIX (ReportSheet.tsx:150, adversarial reviewer). A report that answered 'kept' was never sent again: nothing in apps/classroom/src reads a 'reported' flag back (grep: two references, the union member and the write), so any 503 or offline moment dropped a Safety report for good, and the carefully stamped-once `at` ref — built to make retries idempotent — was dead code. FIX: a 'Try sending it again' button on the 'kept' confirmation only, reusing `send()` and therefore the same `at`, so the relay's idempotency key (user, lessonId, beatId, at) holds and an operator does not read one report twice. I did NOT build the flags.ts sent-marker plus app-open replay the reviewer preferred — see skipped.","SHOULD-FIX (liveClass.ts:194, scope reviewer). The unlocked callers of `prepareLiveClass` were unrecorded. VERIFIED and it is partly a non-issue: `prepareLiveClass`'s `ask` parameter HAS NO `topicRef` field, so there is nothing inside it to lock on and the gate genuinely belongs in the screen. FIX: a docblock above `prepareLiveClass` saying the lock is the caller's job and why, naming the three unlocked callers — screens/CanvasLab.tsx:26 (free-text, no topicRef, could not lock), prep5.ts:95 and prep-clone.ts:33 (operator scripts that can race a live student) — plus a handoff, since all three are foreign.","NOTE (LiveClass.tsx:580, scope reviewer). `await keep(...)` meant a save failure reached the `.catch` and called `setError`, and the error branch renders BEFORE the class — so a failed save would take away a lesson already playing (setDraft fires from `onDraft` during preparation). FIX: the keep is wrapped in its own try/catch with the reason written beside it. The await stays, so the release still follows the write.","IMPROVEMENT (LiveClass.tsx:498, scope reviewer). A second 'held' — a holder demonstrably alive, having renewed through a whole TTL — used to prepare anyway, spending the Rs 30 duplicate the job exists to prevent. FIX: it now routes to the turned-away screen with up to three ready classes, the same honest offer the 'busy' ceiling makes. 'unavailable' still fails OPEN and prepares, exactly per spec, and its comment was corrected because it previously claimed to cover the 'held' case too.","NOTE (LiveClass.tsx:457, adversarial reviewer). On `waitForHolder` returning 'gone' the code re-took the lock without re-reading the catalogue, which the dev/memory prep store makes routine (it never answers 'ready', so the waiter always exits 'gone' the moment the holder releases). FIX: `forgetCatalog()` and a re-check for the topicRef on the 'gone' branch too, navigating to the kept class if it landed.","IMPROVEMENT (ReportSheet.tsx:58 and :313, both reviewers). `MAX_NOTE = 500` was documented as the sheet's own cap while the textarea said `maxLength={400}` — a new docblock asserting a number the file contradicted, which is the shape Wave 2 failed a phase for. FIX: `maxLength={MAX_NOTE}`, so one constant governs the textarea, the local slice and the screen's limit. The docblock now also states the latent truncation honestly: `screen` truncates past its limit where report.ts's rule for this field is reject-never-truncate, it is unreachable from this sheet, and an external caller of the exported `noteThatMayTravel` with a longer note would get a shortened one.","NOTE (ReportSheet.tsx:270, adversarial reviewer). The withheld-note confirmation was written for one of the two verdicts that trigger it: a child whose note tripped the off-limits gate was told to go and tell an adult what happened, when nothing had. FIX: `noteThatMayTravel` now returns `why: 'support' | 'redirect' | null` and the confirmation branches — distress keeps its line, redirect gets 'Some of what you wrote stayed on this phone. The rest of your report has gone.' The withheld paragraph also moved ABOVE 'Somebody will read it', which read as a contradiction in the old order.","IMPROVEMENT (ReportSheet.tsx:219, adversarial reviewer). A withheld distress note reached an operator WORDLESS and carrying whatever kind the child happened to tap — possibly 'other' — so the most serious report this product can receive sorted with the typo complaints. FIX: a new exported `reportKindFor(tapped, why)` files a withheld 'support' note as `kind: 'unsafe'`. Deliberately NOT for 'redirect': that is a boundary, not an emergency, and dressing it as one would put an untrue flag against a child. Four new tests.","judge.ts header, house rule 7. The Rs 5 docblock claimed 'three cheap readings' and leaned on the ~70% figure. Added a paragraph saying what the blocker fix costs: two of the three readings are now refused on an open ask, leaving the WORDS reading and a bare 'I don't know' to carry the whole saving, and the ~70% was measured with all three against a bank that SHOWED its options. It is not a measurement of this path and nothing here should be read as claiming it is."],"skipped":[{"finding":"Gate `fileReport` on consent — `if (!mayPersist()) return 'kept';` at the top of ReportSheet.tsx's send path (scope reviewer note; adversarial JOINT G<->H should-fix)","why":"`mayPersist` is exported from apps/classroom/src/lib/workspace.ts, which is a sibling phase's file and mid-edit RIGHT NOW. I ran apps/classroom/tests/contest-door.test.tsx to check: 4 of its 25 tests currently FAIL, all of them in the sibling's own Gate and trial code (src/App.tsx, lib/workspace.ts). None of my owned files are in that test's import graph. Importing a half-finished foreign module into the Safety #4 path to satisfy a review note would couple a child-safety route to code that does not yet pass its own tests. It is one line whenever the sibling lands; it is a handoff, not an edit. The wider version of the same finding — the Rs 30 preparation at LiveClass.tsx and the ledger writes at ClassRoom.tsx:657/:737 — is the same call for the same reason, and is recorded below."},{"finding":"Replay kept reports: add `sent?: boolean` to stores/flags.ts and re-POST unmarked ones on app open (adversarial should-fix, preferred form)","why":"stores/flags.ts is mine, but the replay has to be triggered from app open, and src/App.tsx is a sibling phase's file and currently dirty. A store field with nothing reading it would be the same dead machinery the finding complains about. I applied the owned half the reviewer offered as the cheaper alternative — the retry button on the 'kept' confirmation, which also gives the child agency — and recorded the replay as a handoff."},{"finding":"Release the lock on `visibilitychange` -> hidden as well as `pagehide` (adversarial should-fix, as worded)","why":"Spec-contrary in effect. Backgrounding is not dying: the preparation keeps running, so releasing on hidden hands the lock to a second student while this browser is still spending, which CAUSES the duplicate Rs 30 the lock exists to prevent. Students background an app constantly. I wired `pagehide` with keepalive, which is the actual unload signal, and wrote the reasoning into the comment beside it rather than silently doing less than was asked."},{"finding":"Put `whyProbe` on `SpokenAsk` and into `describeAsk` so the escalated judge sees the authored options (quality reviewer / builder's own gap)","why":"packages/tutor-judge is foreign to this phase. Recorded as a handoff — and it matters more after my blocker fix than before, because escalation is now the outcome for every ordinal utterance on an open ask."},{"finding":"Export a pure `redact()` from packages/safety so a distressing note can travel scrubbed (builder's own gap, reviewers concur)","why":"packages/safety is foreign. Still the single most valuable open item in this phase: it stands between a child's most serious report and the person who should read it. I narrowed the damage inside owned code instead — the wordless row now files as 'unsafe' so it sorts to the top of the queue."},{"finding":"Reconcile the MVP-2 rollup row: 2.48 + 0.00 + 0.99 = 3.47, published as Rs 3.49 (both reviewers)","why":"docs/prd/4-lam-intelligence.md is foreign. VERIFIED at :1331 — the row prints Rs 3.49 against components that sum to Rs 3.47. Restated correctly here and handed off; no code change was implied."},{"finding":"Thread `tutorLanguage()` through prepareLiveClass/prepareClass (inbound handoff from the sibling Onboarding phase)","why":"Not this phase's spec item, and `prepareClass` is in packages/class-prep, which is foreign. liveClass.ts is mine and would be one end of the thread, but the other end is not. Orchestrator should assign it; the sibling's 'will' wording should stand until it lands."},{"finding":"Correct the builder report's RED count for reportSend (\"8 failed / 2 passed\" should have been 9 failed / 1 passed)","why":"A report-text correction about a run that happened before this session. Nothing to change in the tree, and I cannot re-run a RED against a HEAD version without a git write. Noting it as accepted rather than acting on it."}],"filesTouched":["C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/apps/classroom/src/lib/judge.ts","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/apps/classroom/src/lib/liveClass.ts","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/apps/classroom/src/screens/LiveClass.tsx","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/apps/classroom/src/ui/ReportSheet.tsx","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/apps/classroom/tests/whyProbePlayback.test.ts","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/apps/classroom/tests/prepLock.test.ts","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/apps/classroom/tests/reportSend.test.tsx"],"summary":"Three blockers fixed, eight should-fixes and five cheap improvements applied, eight items skipped with reasons. Every finding was checked against the tree before acting; the two headline blockers reproduced exactly as described, and one reviewer claim was found to be partly wrong.\n\nTHE BLOCKERS WERE REAL AND I REPRODUCED BOTH AS FAILING TESTS FIRST. RED run recorded: `npx vitest run apps/classroom/tests/whyProbePlayback.test.ts` → 5 failed / 10 passed, including 'does NOT read a correct \"four\" as the fourth option — it escalates' returning 'local'. That is a child who answered a four-option open prose probe correctly being told they are wrong, at confidence 1, with no escalation possible — because `readLocally` never read `probe.askedAs` and class-prep/src/prompts.ts:377 tells the planner to emit `askedAs:\"open\"` for every normal ask. The adversarial reviewer's second blocker reproduced in the same run: a correct answer ending 'pata nahi' or 'i am not sure' filed local-WRONG, because match.ts matches the hedge at the start or end of an utterance rather than as the whole of it. GREEN after judge.ts: 15/15. `npx vitest run apps/classroom/tests/prepLock.test.ts` RED 4 failed / 13 passed on the mintPrepId guard, GREEN 17/17. `npx vitest run apps/classroom/tests/reportSend.test.tsx` RED 5 failed / 10 passed, GREEN 15/15.\n\nWHERE A REVIEWER WAS WRONG. The scope reviewer's should-fix asked for the lock to be taken inside `prepareLiveClass` per the spec's literal wording. `prepareLiveClass`'s `ask` parameter has no `topicRef` field at all, and a topicRef is the only key two students can arrive at independently — so there is nothing in there to lock on, and the gate genuinely belongs in the screen that owns the wait and busy states. I took the reviewer's alternative: a docblock saying the lock is the caller's job, naming CanvasLab.tsx:26 (free-text, no topicRef, could not lock even in principle), prep5.ts:95 and prep-clone.ts:33, plus the handoff that was missing. I also declined the `visibilitychange` half of the unload release, in writing: backgrounding is not dying, the preparation keeps running, and releasing there would cause the exact duplicate spend the lock prevents.\n\nWHAT THE FIX COSTS, SAID BEFORE ANYONE ASKS. Refusing the letter and position readings on an open ask removes two of the local path's three readings from the default case. Only the WORDS reading and a bare 'I don't know' now settle an answer without a model, so `f` — the share of spoken answers a whyProbe resolves — is materially LOWER than before this change, and the Rs 3.49 case is correspondingly further away. That is the right trade and it is not free: the alternative was buying the ceiling by telling children who were right that they were wrong. The judge.ts header now says this rather than leaving the ~70% figure standing unqualified; that figure was measured on the placement/quiz bank with all three readings and against a list the student could see, and it was never a measurement of this path. `grep -rl whyProbe content` is still 0, so on committed content the saving remains zero today.\n\nVERIFICATION. `npx vitest run` over the 14 test files that cover the owned paths (broughtPage, classLedger, judge, ledgerReplay, liveclass-screen, migrations, models-allowed, pictureSearch, prepLock, reportSend, safety-wiring, states, sync, whyProbePlayback): 295 passed. File lists, not the directory, per the house rule's spirit. `npx tsc --noEmit` against a scratchpad files-list tsconfig naming the five owned sources plus stores/flags.ts plus the three new tests: clean, no output. `apps/classroom/tests/contest-door.test.tsx` fails 4 of 25 — verified as the sibling phase's own mid-edit Gate/trial code (src/App.tsx, lib/workspace.ts); none of my files are in its import graph. NOT VERIFIED: no browser run. The two changed LiveClass screens (the softened wait copy, the new 'held' turned-away branch) and the changed ReportSheet confirmation have not been seen rendered at 400px.\n\nNO GIT WRITE WAS RUN. Two sibling phases are editing this worktree and one of them currently has failing tests; committing from here would commit their unfinished work under this phase's message. Nothing is staged. The orchestrator should commit once all three phases report.\n\nHANDOFFS, all foreign paths: (1) gate `fileReport` and the LiveClass Rs 30 preparation on the sibling's `mayPersist()` once lib/workspace.ts is green — the pre-consent trial currently creates a student_report row and pays for a preparation for a person with no consent record; (2) export a pure `redact()` from packages/safety so a distressing note can travel scrubbed instead of withheld; (3) put `whyProbe` into tutor-judge's `describeAsk`, which matters more now that escalation is the default for ordinal utterances; (4) give `createBrowserLlm`/`relayFetch` a way to carry x-prep-id so `usage_event.prep_id` attributes the spend and not just the zero-cost control calls; (5) export `mint` from stores/ledger.ts so liveClass.ts's `mintPrepId` is not a second copy; (6) reconcile the PRD's MVP-2 row (components sum to Rs 3.47, printed as Rs 3.49); (7) thread `tutorLanguage()` through prepareClass; (8) measure the whyProbe survival rate out of `prepare.ts` and the local-match rate on real class asks — that number, not this wiring, decides whether the judge line is Rs 0.00 or Rs 5.61."},"fixH":{"phase":"H-contest-door — enrolment, consent, and the workspace it builds (review fixes applied)","applied":["BLOCKER (all three reviewers) — mayPersist() stale-consent hole. apps/classroom/src/lib/workspace.ts:219 is now `return !isTrialSession();`. The old `readWorkspace().consent !== null` second clause was dead in the flow it was written for (markConsented calls endTrial BEFORE writeWorkspace, so a consented student is never in a trial) and live in exactly the dangerous one: a second child on a shared kiosk or family phone, whose new tab clears the sessionStorage trial mark but keeps the first child's localStorage consent. Reproduced as a RED test first (tests/workspace.test.ts, 'does not let an earlier student’s consent unlock a new trial on the same device' — failed 'expected true to be false', now passes). The docblock now explains why no such clause can exist.","BLOCKER (reviewers 1+2) — the identified screen_viewed during a trial, in an owned file. apps/classroom/src/App.tsx Boundaried's effect now returns early when !mayPersist() before track('screen_viewed'), so the stable distinct_id (lib/telemetry.ts:284) is not emitted for /try or any trial screen. The comment names it as the one such writer inside this phase's ownership and keeps telemetry.ts's own early return as the standing handoff.","BLOCKER (reviewers 2+3) — the trial Gate bypass that put a Rs-30 preparation two taps from a screen promising it would never happen. apps/classroom/src/App.tsx Gate no longer short-circuits on isTrialSession() for every path. A trial that has not onboarded is now confined to isTrialPath(): /syllabus, /chapter/<id>/<id>, /lesson/<id>, /try, /enrol — anything else redirects to /try, never to /onboarding (which would ask a child for their name). /class/* (prepareClass), /settings (a synced preference write), /diagnostic and /progress are therefore unreachable for a pre-consent minor. The predicate lives in lib/workspace.ts (isTrialPath, TRIAL_HOME) so the Gate and the trial screen cannot drift; TrialConsent's own trialDestination now calls it instead of keeping a private copy. /chapter is included deliberately — it is the catalogue's only route into a lesson (Syllabus.tsx:179), and without it every tap in a trial would bounce. Covered by three new tests.","BLOCKER (reviewer 2) — the consent artifact stated a falsehood. apps/classroom/src/screens/contest/Enrol.tsx consent step no longer says 'no words a student types or speaks leave their device'. Verified against the tree: lib/ear.ts:205 posts the raw WAV of every spoken answer to /api/hear and its own docblock says 'the relay hears everything'; typed doubts go to the relay to be answered. The sentence now says what happens — the child's speech and questions are sent to us to be understood and answered, not sold, not handed on, not used to work out who they are. A new test asserts the consent step makes no stays-on-the-device claim, so the sentence cannot come back.","BLOCKER (reviewers 1+2+3) — Class 12 could not enrol and the screen said it could. Verified: packages/syllabus/data holds classes 6-11 only, subjectsAvailable(12) is [], the old Continue guard (`subjects.length === 0 && available.length > 0`) let an empty selection through, and the relay refuses it at enroll.ts:415-421 with a 400 the client shows verbatim on the code screen after the SMS was spent. Fixed by a new exported subjectsFor(klass) in Enrol.tsx: an authored class offers its own subjects, an unauthored one offers the ordinary NCERT list (Physics/Chemistry/Mathematics/Biology for 11+, Mathematics/Science below) so the student picks their own answer rather than a placeholder being posted for them. The guard is now `disabled={subjects.length === 0}` for every class, the false comment ('must still get through') is replaced with what actually happens, and the copy says lessons for that class are still being written instead of 'you can still enrol'. New test walks all seven classes on the door and asserts each has something to pick.","SHOULD-FIX (reviewers 1+2+3) — the 'bad-code' refusal for a phone problem. lib/enroll.ts submitEnrolment now returns a new EnrolRefusal kind 'bad-phone' with phone wording, instead of telling a parent whose number failed toE164 to ask for a new SMS and spend sends against the 3/phone/10min gate. RED test first (tests/enrolClient.test.ts: 'names the phone when the phone is the problem, and never sends it' — failed 'expected bad-code to be bad-phone').","SHOULD-FIX (reviewer 3, reproduced) — toE164 truncated 91-series mobiles. `digits.replace(/^(?:91|0)/,'')` turned 9198765432 (an allocated ten-digit mobile) into eight digits and told a parent their own number was not a number, with Continue dead under it. Now strips by LENGTH: 10 digits as-is, 11 only with a leading 0, 12 only with a leading 91. RED test first; four 91-series cases now asserted.","SHOULD-FIX (reviewer 3) — the 429 dead end. Enrol.tsx sendCode now advances to the 'otp' step on a 'rate-limited' or 'gate-down' refusal, keeping the wait sentence above the input. The send gate is 10 minutes and a code lives 10 minutes, so the old behaviour blocked the code for exactly as long as the code was good for. Advancing sends no SMS.","SHOULD-FIX (reviewer 1) — JOB 3's tutor language was never asked or written on the contest door, unrecoverably (the finish() settingsStore write makes isOnboarded true, so the Gate never routes that student to onboarding's language step and there is no Settings control). Added 'language' as an eighth ENROL_STEP between subjects and school, reusing Onboarding's LANGUAGE_QUESTION, and finish() now calls setTutorLanguage(). Test updated to the new step list.","SHOULD-FIX (reviewers 2+3) — the tutor-language copy promised behaviour nothing implements. Verified by grep: tutorLanguage() has no consumer in apps/classroom/src. Both language steps now say 'the voice I will teach you in', that every class written so far is in English, and that Hinglish arrives with the ones being written — with a WHY comment carrying the handoff and the fact that packages/tutor-judge matchSpokenAnswer(said, options) (match.ts:124) takes no language argument, so it is not a one-liner for phase G.","IMPROVEMENT (reviewer 3) — contestCode is trimmed and bounded once, in campaignOf(). `/enrol?c=` no longer makes the local consent mark say 'contest' while the relay records 'self_serve', and a campaign over the relay's 40-character bound (enroll.ts:446-448) is dropped rather than sent to a 400 that would reach a parent as 'your code is wrong'.","NOTE (reviewer 2) — the OTP screen no longer says 'six digits'. Six is the dev provider's length; the relay accepts four to eight (enroll.ts:443) so a vendor can send another.","NOTE (reviewer 2) — the deletion sentence now names the only channel that exists (whoever gave them the link), with a NOT VERIFIED comment recording that the repo has no grievance address at all, which PRD 3 and PRD 6 both log as the same zero-hit grep.","NOTE (reviewer 1) — endTrial()'s docblock no longer claims a caller it does not have ('and on leaving the trial'); it names markConsented as its one caller.","IMPROVEMENT (reviewer 2) — the two trial tests that regexed prose are renamed to say they assert the rendered words, with a docblock saying they certify no behaviour; a third now asserts the screen does NOT claim marks are already unkept.","Cheap owned improvement — a bare /try used to send a trial to the catalogue, whose only button opened Settings; the Gate now refuses that, which turns a write into a dead end. TrialConsent says so on the screen when the link named no class, rather than letting a child discover it. Test covers both the bare and the ?next=/lesson/<id> case.","Honesty edit — my own new App.tsx comment no longer asserts Rs 30 as the preparation cost: the repo carries two figures 13x apart (Rs 30 at docs/prd/6-cost-monetisation-ops.md:573, ₹2.25 at lib/content.ts:68) and the comment now names both and neither as the number."],"skipped":[{"finding":"Reviewer 2's preferred fix for blocker 3: do not ship /try at all until the mayPersist() guards land in telemetry, progress, ledger, model and prepareClass.","why":"Every one of those guards is in a file this phase does not own (house rule 3), so landing them first is not something this phase can do, and deleting the route would discard JOB 2's deliverable on my own judgement. Took the alternative the same reviewer sanctioned: the Gate is confined to the allow-list the screen computes, and the copy moved off the present tense for everything not enforced. FLAG FOR THE ORCHESTRATOR — if the preference is to hold the door until the guards are in, that is a one-line route removal in App.tsx and the screen is ready for it."},{"finding":"Reviewers 1 and 3's first option for Class 12: build CLASSES from `k => subjectsAvailable(k).length > 0`, i.e. drop unauthored classes from the door.","why":"Both reviewers offered it as one of two acceptable fixes and reviewer 3 named the other as better. Dropping class 12 closes the segment with the most intent on the funnel's only front door, for a content gap rather than a product one. Took the subjectsFor() route instead: the student picks their own subjects, the relay stores an answer a person actually gave, and the copy is honest that the lessons are still being written."},{"finding":"Reviewer 1's fix for the trial's happy path: point DEFAULT_TRIAL_DESTINATION at a concrete already-prepared lesson id from content/index.json.","why":"The lesson ids live in content/, which this phase does not own, and hard-coding one into a screen makes a marketing destination that rots silently the day that lesson is renamed. The harm the finding named (landing on a screen whose button writes a synced preference) is removed anyway — the Gate refuses a trial /settings. What is left is a dead end, and the screen now says so out loud. Handoff stands: Syllabus.tsx taking a session-scoped class makes the bare link work."},{"finding":"Reviewer 2's second half of the test finding: add behavioural tests that begin a trial, call the writers, and assert nothing reached progressStore / the ledger / track's payload.","why":"Those writers do not consult mayPersist() yet and live in foreign files, so such a test would fail by construction today and could only be written as a skipped one. The one writer inside this phase's ownership (App.tsx screen_viewed) was fixed instead, and the prose tests were renamed so nobody reads 45 green as proof the trial is silent."},{"finding":"Reviewer 2's note asking the deletion sentence to name an email address.","why":"There is no address to name. A repo-wide grep for mailto:/support@/grievance returns zero hits, which PRD 3:233 and PRD 6:313 both record. Naming one would be inventing a channel. The sentence now points at the only channel that exists and the code comment records the gap as NOT VERIFIED."},{"finding":"Reviewer 1's note on Enrol.tsx's `initialStep` prop.","why":"The reviewer's own verdict is 'no change required' — it is the seam the render-to-string tests enter at, and @testing-library/react is still not a dependency of this repo."},{"finding":"Everything in the relayed user request about saving to git ('SB KUCH GIT PR SAVE KR DO').","why":"House rule 1 forbids any git write from this phase, and two sibling phases have half-finished edits in this same tree right now (judge.ts, liveClass.ts, ClassRoom.tsx, LiveClass.tsx, ReportSheet.tsx are theirs, mid-flight) — a commit from here would sweep them up. Reads only: git status --short confirms this phase's edits are confined to its own paths. The save is the orchestrator's to make once all three phases report."}],"filesTouched":["C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/apps/classroom/src/lib/workspace.ts","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/apps/classroom/src/lib/enroll.ts","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/apps/classroom/src/screens/contest/Enrol.tsx","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/apps/classroom/src/screens/contest/TrialConsent.tsx","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/apps/classroom/src/screens/Onboarding.tsx","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/apps/classroom/src/App.tsx","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/apps/classroom/tests/workspace.test.ts","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/apps/classroom/tests/enrolClient.test.ts","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/apps/classroom/tests/contest-door.test.tsx"],"summary":"All five blockers and every should-fix are applied inside owned paths; three skips are argued above, one of them a sequencing decision the orchestrator may want to overturn.\n\nVERIFICATION FIRST, because reviewers are fallible too. Every claim I acted on was checked against the tree: packages/syllabus/data does hold classes 6-11 only; ear.ts:205 does post the student's WAV to /api/hear under a docblock reading 'the relay hears everything'; markConsented does call endTrial before writeWorkspace, which is what made mayPersist's consent clause dead-except-when-dangerous; Syllabus.tsx:98 is the klass === null branch whose only button opens Settings; matchSpokenAnswer(said, options) at packages/tutor-judge/src/match.ts:124 does take no language argument; and grep confirms tutorLanguage() still has no consumer. Two reviewer suggestions I did NOT take as written because the tree disagreed with the shape of them are in the skipped list with the reason.\n\nTDD, with the RED run recorded. `npx vitest run apps/classroom/tests/workspace.test.ts apps/classroom/tests/enrolClient.test.ts apps/classroom/tests/contest-door.test.tsx` → 3 files failed, 7 tests failed / 34 passed, including 'expected true to be false' on the shared-device mayPersist case and 'expected bad-code to be bad-phone' on the unparseable phone. After the implementation the same command is GREEN at 45 tests (up from 35: four new, two renamed, one step-list assertion updated). The neighbouring stores were re-run untouched — 7 files, 101 tests passed. Per-file tsc over all six owned source files and all three test files exits 0 with no diagnostics; `npm run build`, `npm run typecheck` and a bare full-suite run were never invoked (house rule 2). No git write, no network call, no migration, no paid API, no pre-warm runner.\n\nWHAT CHANGED IN SUBSTANCE. (1) mayPersist() is now `!isTrialSession()` — a consent mark on a device can no longer unlock persistence for the next child at a shared kiosk. (2) A trial is confined by the Gate to the allow-list the trial screen already computed, shared from lib/workspace.ts as isTrialPath, so /class/* — the preparation, and the only path that spends money — is unreachable rather than two taps away, and /settings, /diagnostic and /progress close to a pre-consent minor at the same time. (3) The one identified analytics writer inside this phase's ownership is guarded. (4) The DPDP consent artifact no longer tells a parent that words stay on the device while the app posts them to the relay. (5) Class 12 can enrol, with subjects it chose, instead of burning an SMS to be told 'subjects is 1 to 10 short names' on the code screen forever.\n\nWHAT REMAINS UNTRUE ON A SCREEN: nothing, deliberately. The trial's promise list now contains only what code in this bundle keeps (no new class, nobody to tell, no rest-of-app, the tab forgets), and a separate box says in plain words that the not-saving part is NOT FINISHED and may still be written down on the device. That is worse copy than it was and it is the only version that is true today. The handoffs that would let those lines move into the kept list are unchanged and still accurate to the line: telemetry.ts:284 track, ledger.ts:155/168, model.ts:53, progress.ts:21/31, liveClass.ts:240 prepareClass, sync.ts:92 connectSync, plus ui/ReportSheet.tsx's send path (NOT VERIFIED — that file is mid-edit by a sibling phase this wave) and stores/profile.ts declaring `workspace?: Workspace` so lib/workspace.ts can drop its cast.\n\nONE THING THE ORCHESTRATOR SHOULD DECIDE, NOT ME: whether /try ships at all before those guards land. I took the confine-and-tell-the-truth route because holding the route would discard a deliverable on my own judgement and the guards are in files I may not touch; removing the route is one line in App.tsx if the answer is the other way.\n\nAlso flagged for the wave: the repo carries two preparation costs 13x apart — Rs 30 a topic at docs/prd/6-cost-monetisation-ops.md:573 and ₹2.25 at apps/classroom/src/lib/content.ts:68. My new comments name both rather than picking one. Somebody owns reconciling them, and the Rs 5/student-hour ceiling arithmetic in this phase's earlier report rests on the larger."},"buildI":{"phase":"I-lipsync-spike","filesTouched":["C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/README.md","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/lib/format.mjs","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/lib/format.d.mts","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/lib/sentences.mjs","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/lib/sentences.d.mts","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/lib/scale.mjs","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/lib/scale.d.mts","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/lib/rules.mjs","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/lib/rules.d.mts","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/lib/metrics.mjs","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/lib/metrics.d.mts","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/lib/rules-driver.mjs","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/lib/rules-driver.d.mts","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/lib/rhubarb-driver.mjs","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/lib/audio.mjs","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/lib/corpus.mjs","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/lib/dictionary.mjs","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/bin/lipsync.mjs","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/bin/compare.mjs","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/corpus/hinglish.json","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/vendor/package.json","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/vendor/package-lock.json","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/docs/research/lipsync-hinglish-result.md"],"testsAddedOrChanged":["C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/tests/format.test.ts — 13 tests: the nine shapes and their commission notes, overlap resolution on cues copied verbatim from a real rhubarb-wasm run, same-shape run merging, gap filling with idle, clamping past end-of-audio, throwing on a tenth shape, validateTimeline problem reporting, measured (not quoted) byte size","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/tests/sentences.test.ts — 7 tests: full stop / question / exclamation splitting, the Devanagari danda, NOT splitting a decimal (\"5.5 litres\"), NOT splitting the em dash the narration is full of, empty input, unterminated tail","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/tests/scale.test.ts — 9 tests: end-to-end laying of unitless weights, exact summation to the clip duration with the last cue absorbing rounding, start offset, weight proportionality, empty input; allocate() proportional split and exact summation","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/tests/rules.test.ts — 14 tests: lips shut on P/B/M, teeth-on-lip for F/V, tongue for L, pucker for W and OO, EE as clenched teeth, the `ear`/`ar` rules, only-the-nine output, no back-to-back repeats, empty word, idle in word gaps, comma pause > space pause, all weights positive, every one of the nine weighted, Hinglish does not throw","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/tests/metrics.test.ts — 11 tests: midpoint sampling, idle where the timeline is silent, zero/one disagreement bounds, shape-vs-open/shut separation, cue count and mean hold, shut fraction including uncovered time, bytes-per-minute, gzipped bytes","C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/tests/rules-driver.test.ts — 6 tests, ADDED AFTER the implementation (the file says so in its own docblock): valid timeline for English and for Hinglish, zero-to-end gapless coverage, scaling with clip length, the lead-ms lever, refusal on no text or no clock"],"commandsRun":[{"cmd":"npx vitest run tools/lipsync/tests","outcome":"RED, as required before implementation — 5 test files failed to collect, 'no tests', every one 'Failed to resolve import ../lib/*.mjs. Does the file exist?'. The five lib modules did not exist yet."},{"cmd":"npx vitest run tools/lipsync/tests (after writing lib/format.mjs, sentences.mjs, scale.mjs, rules.mjs, metrics.mjs)","outcome":"GREEN — 5 files, 52 tests passed."},{"cmd":"npx vitest run tools/lipsync/tests/metrics.test.ts (gzip metric, test written first)","outcome":"RED — 1 failed, 10 passed: 'TypeError: actual value must be number or bigint, received undefined' at expect(m.gzipBytes). Then GREEN after adding gzipBytes/gzipBytesPerMinute to clipMetrics."},{"cmd":"npx vitest run tools/lipsync/tests (final)","outcome":"GREEN — 6 files, 59 tests passed, 5.33s. No other test path was run; the whole-workspace suite was deliberately not invoked."},{"cmd":"npm install --prefix tools/lipsync/vendor rhubarb-lip-sync-wasm@0.1.8 --no-audit --no-fund","outcome":"added 1 package in 4s (19s on the first, cold, scratchpad probe). NO native build, no compiler, no node-gyp. Root package.json and package-lock.json verified unmodified afterwards via `git diff --stat`."},{"cmd":"node tools/lipsync/bin/compare.mjs --out <scratchpad>/lipsync-results.json","outcome":"Full 4-arm run over all six class10-blood clips (121.76s audio) plus dictionary coverage. ~5 minutes wall. Every number in the research doc is a column of this output. Re-run after adding the gzip column."},{"cmd":"ffmpeg -i content/class10-blood/audio/hook.m4a -ac 1 -ar 16000 -f s16le ... ; node smoke.mjs","outcome":"First rhubarb-wasm smoke test: 19.712s of PCM aligned in 27.9s, 138 mouth cues. This run is where the overlapping-cue defect was spotted in the raw output."},{"cmd":"node -e '<rhubarb with Hinglish dialogText over English audio>'","outcome":"Did not throw, did not warn. 119 cues vs 127 for the correct English script; 15.3% shape disagreement — statistically indistinguishable from the 12.4% that NO script produced on the same clip. The dialogText bias simply evaporates."},{"cmd":"node -e '<extract cmudict-en-us.dict from rhubarb.data at the packer manifest offsets>'","outcome":"125,945 unique words, 134,723 entries. Probe confirmed hai/dekho/khoon/nahi/chalo absent; 'beta' present but as the Greek letter."},{"cmd":"npx tsc --noEmit --ignoreConfig --strict --target ES2022 --module ESNext --moduleResolution bundler --skipLibCheck --lib ES2022,DOM --types node tools/lipsync/tests/*.test.ts","outcome":"First run RED with TS7016 'could not find a declaration file for ../lib/*.mjs'. Comparing against tools/prewarm revealed the repo's established .d.mts sidecar pattern; added six sidecars and type-annotated the test fixtures. Final run CLEAN, no output. `npm run typecheck` was NOT run (house rule 2); this narrow per-file invocation names only my own files."},{"cmd":"node tools/lipsync/bin/lipsync.mjs --shapes / --check","outcome":"--shapes prints the nine-shape commission brief; --check reports 'ffmpeg yes' and 'rhubarb yes — tools/lipsync/vendor/node_modules/rhubarb-lip-sync-wasm'."},{"cmd":"git status --porcelain","outcome":"Read only. Confirms the only paths I created are tools/lipsync/ and docs/research/lipsync-hinglish-result.md. No git write of any kind was run."}],"contracts":["The one format, unchanged from avatar-3.md:208 — `[{ t: number, d: number, v: 'A'|'B'|'C'|'D'|'E'|'F'|'G'|'H'|'X' }]`, start/duration/shape in seconds. Always ordered, gapless, non-overlapping, covering exactly [0, duration]. Typed as `MouthCue` in tools/lipsync/lib/format.d.mts.","`MOUTH_SHAPES: MouthShape[]` and `SHAPE_NOTES: Record<MouthShape, string>` from tools/lipsync/lib/format.mjs — this pair IS the illustrator's commission brief. Nine drawings over one locked face. Printable with `node tools/lipsync/bin/lipsync.mjs --shapes`.","`normaliseCues(raw: RawCue[], options?: { duration?: number, padStart?: boolean }): MouthCue[]` — the ONLY sanctioned way to turn an aligner's {start,end,value} into the format. Throws on a shape outside the nine. Any consumer of rhubarb output that skips this pass ships the overlap defect.","`alignByRules(text: string, durationSec: number, options?: { leadMs?: number }): MouthCue[]` — the text-only fallback driver. Returns [] on empty text or zero duration; [] is a refusal, never a valid mouth.","`alignByRhubarb(pcm: Buffer, options?: { dialogText?: string, duration?: number }): Promise<{ cues: MouthCue[], raw: RawCue[], elapsedMs: number }>` — pcm MUST be 16 kHz mono s16le, which `decodePcm16k(path)` in tools/lipsync/lib/audio.mjs produces. rhubarb-lip-sync-wasm accepts nothing else.","`clipMetrics(cues, duration): ClipMetrics` and `disagreement(a, b, { rateHz, duration }): Disagreement` — the observable-signal vocabulary. There is deliberately NO `accuracy` field on either type; adding one is a lie until a clip is hand-labelled.","Optional-dependency resolution order for the aligner: `CURIE_RHUBARB_DIR` env var, then `tools/lipsync/vendor/node_modules/rhubarb-lip-sync-wasm`, then root node_modules, then bare specifier. The aligner is NOT in the root package.json by design."],"costEffect":"NONE on Rs/student-hour, and the arithmetic is worth having because this phase existed to protect a spend.\n\nWHAT IT ADDS. Alignment is a laptop build step: no API call, no GPU, no per-minute fee, no relay traffic. Measured throughput 1.12-1.15x real time single-threaded, so a 12-minute lesson costs about 13.8 minutes of one laptop core, once, forever.\n\nWHAT THE STUDENT DOWNLOADS. Measured, gzipped, because every byte of this ships gzipped: 2.06 KB/min on the Rhubarb path, 2.63 KB/min on the rule table. The six class10-blood beats are 121.76s, so 2.06 x (121.76/60) = 4.18 KB of mouth data alongside 497 KB of .m4a narration (508,915 bytes of audio measured on disk) — 0.84% of the audio's own weight. A full 12-minute lesson is 2.06 x 12 = ~25 KB. Runtime is reading an array: no analyser, no FFT, no per-frame CPU.\n\nA CORRECTION TO THE RESEARCH DOC'S OWN NUMBER: avatar-3.md:208 estimates \"roughly 2 KB per minute\". That holds GZIPPED (2.06 measured) and is wrong by 5-8x RAW (9.96-16.66 KB/min on disk). Quote the gzipped figure; the raw one would look alarming and would be the wrong number.\n\nWHAT IT AVOIDS, which is the actual economics. avatar-3.md prices Mascotbot — the commercial version of exactly this — at about $0.04 per minute of rendered speech, i.e. $2.40 per hour of tutor speech. The ceiling is Rs 5 per student-hour, cash, everything in. At any exchange rate of this century $2.40 is not inside Rs 5 and is not close. This spike neither improved nor damaged that gap; it confirmed the browser-rendered stylised route still clears it.\n\nMONEY SPENT THIS PHASE: zero. No paid API was called, no TTS was rendered, the pre-warm runner was not touched, and no Supabase migration was pushed. The only download was an MIT npm package.","honestGaps":["THE HEADLINE GAP, and no work inside this phase could have closed it: Rhubarb's ACOUSTIC performance on Hinglish audio is NOT MEASURED. There is no Hinglish audio in this repository — `find` over the tree returns only the six English class10-blood .m4a files and their two build copies. Rendering Hinglish narration costs a real Gemini TTS call, which this phase did not make. Everything I measured about Hinglish concerns the DICTIONARY; the acoustic model's behaviour on retroflex stops, aspirates and the Hindi vowel inventory is untested. My own arm 2 result (script bias contributes only 15.2% even in English, so acoustics dominate) means this untested half is the LARGER half of the question.","NOT MEASURED: whether either mouth reads as alive. No mouth was rendered and no human watched one. Every number produced is a cue statistic, and cue statistics have never made a character believable. avatar-3.md:239 already prescribes the fix (nine placeholder mouths played against the real audio, watched for an afternoon) and I did not do it, because it is not a thing a test can do and because drawing is explicitly out of scope for this phase.","NOT MEASURED: native Rhubarb with `-r phonetic`. Not attempted — it needs a C++ build on Windows. The brief's own instruction ('if it cannot, do NOT fight it') applies. So my claim that the phonetic recogniser is unreachable is a claim about the WASM PACKAGE (verified by reading its API surface, its README, and the ten files in its Emscripten manifest), NOT a claim that the phonetic recogniser performs badly on Hinglish. Nobody has run it.","NOT MEASURED: any device cost. Nothing ran on a phone. 'Runtime is reading an array' is reasoning from the algorithm, not a benchmark.","The 48.2% Hinglish OOV figure is a FLOOR, not a point estimate. A dictionary hit is not a correct pronunciation — 'beta' scores as covered because the Greek letter is in CMUdict (B EY T AH), while the word a tutor says to a child is a different word entirely. The true damage is worse than 48.2% by an unmeasured margin.","The hand-written Hinglish corpus (tools/lipsync/corpus/hinglish.json) is MY prose, not a transcript and not generated. It is one author's idea of the tutor register, six beats of one lesson. A different author would write different sentences and the OOV rate would move. The file's own first field says all of this at length.","The 80.3% rules-en vs rules-hi disagreement is NOT a degradation measurement and the doc says so explicitly. They are different sentences with different words; of course the mouths differ. What that arm actually supports is the weaker, true claim that the rule table does not collapse on Hinglish (9.38 vs 8.39 cues/s, 27.1% vs 28.0% shut).","The rule table's sentence allocation gives each sentence a share of the clip proportional to how much MOUTH MOVEMENT it contains, not how long it was actually SPOKEN. Those are different quantities. Per-sentence splitting bounds the resulting drift but does not remove it. Named in the file that causes it (lib/sentences.mjs) as well as in the report.","tools/lipsync/tests/rules-driver.test.ts was written AFTER its implementation, not test-first — it is a contract test added when the measurement run made clear the composition was unpinned. The six modules under it were genuinely TDD'd with recorded RED runs. The file's own docblock admits this rather than letting a reviewer discover it.","The harness output JSON lives only in my scratchpad; I did not commit a results.json into the repo. Every table in the research doc is therefore reproducible (§9 gives the three commands) but not independently auditable without a ~5-minute re-run. A deliberate choice against committing a build artefact, but a reviewer should know the raw run is not in the tree.","The per-shape relative duration weights in lib/rules.mjs are hand-set in the spirit of TalkingHead's own table. They are NOT derived from this repo's audio and nothing claims they are. Fitting them to measured phone durations is unexplored work."],"handoffs":["GIT — THE USER'S OWN REQUEST, WHICH I DID NOT ACT ON. The relayed request was 'SB KUCH GIT PR SAVE KR DO OR WAVE 3 SHURU KRO'. House rule 1 forbids every git write from a phase agent sharing this tree with two concurrent phases, so I ran no add, commit, branch or push. My work is entirely untracked and ready: `tools/lipsync/` and `docs/research/lipsync-hinglish-result.md` — nothing else. Verified with `git status --porcelain`: the only other dirty paths belong to sibling phases. Whoever holds the commit lock must stage these two paths. NOTE that `tools/lipsync/vendor/node_modules/` (37 MB) is already excluded by the repo-wide `node_modules/` gitignore rule; `tools/lipsync/vendor/package.json` and `package-lock.json` (two small files) ARE meant to be committed.","docs/research/README.md — FOREIGN FILE, NOT EDITED. Its table indexes every research note (recon-0..5, avatar-0..3) and my new note is not in it. Someone who owns that file should add a row: `| lipsync-hinglish-result.md | Whether the cheap build-step lip-sync pipeline survives Hinglish — measured on the repo's only rendered audio |`. Its header also reads 'Research notes, 2026-08-23', which my note post-dates.","THE NEXT ACTION IS A HUMAN ONE, NOT A CODE ONE. Before any further aligner work, someone must draw nine ugly placeholder mouths (rectangles are fine) and play them against content/class10-blood/audio in a canvas, both drivers side by side, and answer whether the mouth belongs to the voice. Watch specifically at the 35.8% open/shut disagreement — that is where the two pipelines visibly diverge and a person settles it in ten seconds. If the rule table survives that watch, the recogniser question is closed forever and no aligner is ever needed. If it does not, the next spend is Hinglish AUDIO (render one beat) before it is a heavier aligner.","THE ART CAN BE COMMISSIONED NOW, and this is the phase's operative output. The nine shapes are fixed by the FORMAT, not by the aligner, and both drivers emit the same nine — so no measurement here can invalidate the commission. Brief: one head-and-shoulders illustration, nine registered mouth drawings over one locked face, two eye states, a breathing idle, with A->C->D and C->E->F reading smoothly because C and E double as in-betweens. `node tools/lipsync/bin/lipsync.mjs --shapes` prints it. Registration of the nine mouths over a single locked face is the part avatar-3.md:201 says AI image models reliably get wrong — keep it a small human job.","FOR WHOEVER BUILDS THE RENDERER (phase G or later): consume `MouthCue` from tools/lipsync/lib/format.d.mts and NEVER consume rhubarb-lip-sync-wasm's raw output directly. Measured: 168 of its 794 raw cues (21.2%) violate the timeline contract — they overlap each other and arrive out of order. normaliseCues() resolves it at the boundary. Skipping that pass means telling the renderer to draw two mouths at the same instant.","PLANNING CORRECTION TO avatar-3.md, which its own readers will otherwise act on: its recommended Hinglish mitigation (`-r phonetic`, section 1A) is NOT REACHABLE from its recommended package (rhubarb-lip-sync-wasm, section 1B). That package's entire API is getLipSync(pcm, dialogText) with no recogniser selector, and its payload contains one acoustic model and one English dictionary. You get the npm convenience OR the phonetic recogniser, never both. I did not edit avatar-3.md — it is not my path — so this correction currently lives only in my note (§2).","COST/BANDWIDTH OWNER: adds ~25 KB gzipped per 12-minute lesson to what a student downloads, or 0.84% of the narration audio it accompanies. Zero runtime compute, zero API spend. Nothing here moves the Rs 5/student-hour ceiling."],"summary":"Built tools/lipsync/ and ran the measurement avatar-3.md demanded before any art is commissioned. Six TDD'd modules (RED runs recorded), two drivers behind one format, a four-arm comparison harness, 59 passing tests, and docs/research/lipsync-hinglish-result.md. No art, no renderer, no money spent.\n\nTHE ANSWER. The pipeline SHAPE survives Hinglish and the art can be commissioned today — the nine mouth shapes are fixed by the format, not the aligner, and both drivers emit the same nine. The RECOGNISER does not survive, but not for the reason the brief expected, and the real reason is worse.\n\nTHE FINDING THAT CHANGES THE PLAN. avatar-3.md:1A names `-r phonetic` as the Hinglish mitigation. That fallback IS NOT REACHABLE from rhubarb-lip-sync-wasm. Its entire API is getLipSync(pcm, dialogText) — no recogniser selector — and its Emscripten manifest contains one acoustic model and one English dictionary. The plan does not compose: npm convenience or the phonetic recogniser, never both.\n\nTHE HARD NUMBER. I extracted the CMU dictionary from rhubarb.data at the packer's own byte offsets: 125,945 words. English narration OOV 0.8% (2 tokens). Hinglish OOV 48.2% (134 of 278 tokens), 40-65% per beat. Nearly half of every Hinglish sentence is a word the recogniser has never heard of — and they are the grammatical skeleton (hai, dekho, khoon, nahi, chalo), while the English technical nouns it code-switches into are all present. Two caveats that both cut the same way: a hit is not a correct pronunciation ('beta' is in there as the Greek letter), so 48.2% is a FLOOR; and the two English misses are 'litres' and 'haemoglobin' — this repo's own British spelling rule puts the blood lesson's most important word out of vocabulary against a US dictionary.\n\nWHAT THE FOUR ARMS SHOWED (121.76s, six clips, 100 Hz sampling). rhubarb+dialog 6.00 cues/s, 167 ms mean hold, 21.2% shut, 1.15x real time. rules-hi 9.38 cues/s, 27.1% shut, instant. Disagreements: rhubarb-with-script vs without 15.2% shape / 3.0% open-shut; rhubarb vs rules 77.4% / 35.8%. A fifth arm: feeding Rhubarb a Hinglish script over English audio does not crash and lands 15.3% from the correct script — indistinguishable from the 12.4% that NO script produced. The dialogText bias does not poison anything; it silently stops helping.\n\nNO ACCURACY NUMBER APPEARS ANYWHERE, and the harness prints a paragraph saying why. Nothing is hand-labelled, so there is no answer to compare against. Every column is a fact about the output.\n\nA DEFECT ANY RENDERER WILL INHERIT: 168 of rhubarb-wasm's 794 raw cues (21.2%) overlap each other and arrive out of order. normaliseCues() resolves it once at the boundary; the tests pin it against cues copied verbatim from a real run.\n\nTHE HONEST GAP, which is the largest thing here: Rhubarb's ACOUSTIC behaviour on Hinglish is unmeasured, because there is no Hinglish audio in this repo and rendering some costs a real TTS call. Since the arms show acoustics dominate the dictionary, that untested half is the bigger half. The next step is a human watching nine placeholder mouths against the real audio — a thing no test can do."},"scopeI":{"phase":"I-lipsync-spike","lens":"SCOPE","findings":[{"severity":"should-fix","file":"C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/docs/research/lipsync-hinglish-result.md","issue":"DROPPED SPEC ITEM. The brief names exactly one thing the doc must contain beyond the measurement: \"what the nine mouth shapes are, because that list is what the illustrator gets commissioned against.\" The list is not in the doc. The word \"nine\" appears eleven times and §7 (line 239) says \"Commission the art. Now\", but grep for the shape descriptions (`puckered`, `teeth clenched`, `upper teeth on lower lip`, `tongue raised`) returns no match anywhere in the file. The list exists only in tools/lipsync/README.md:27-37 and tools/lipsync/lib/format.mjs:28,38-49, and line 318 punts the reader to the README. This doc is the artefact that reaches whoever briefs the illustrator; as written it tells them to commission art and then sends them to two other files to find out what to commission.","fix":"Paste the nine-row table from tools/lipsync/README.md:27-37 into §7 directly under the commission paragraph, along with the C-and-E-double-as-in-betweens constraint (A->C->D, C->E->F). It is nine lines and it is the one deliverable the brief singled out."},{"severity":"should-fix","file":"C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/bin/compare.mjs","issue":"A LOAD-BEARING ARM IS OUTSIDE THE HARNESS. The doc's \"fifth arm\" (lines 136-147) — Hinglish dialogText over English audio, 119 cues, 15.3% disagreement — is the evidence for the conclusion \"A script the dictionary cannot match does not poison the recogniser; it simply stops helping\", which feeds the §7 recommendation directly. It was produced by an ad-hoc `node -e` one-liner recorded only in the builder report. compare.mjs runs four arms and the pairs list at compare.mjs:126-130 has no entry for it, so §9's reproduction recipe regenerates every other number in the doc but not this one. Spec item 2 asked for a comparison harness that reports the arms; this arm is not in it.","fix":"Add the arm in compare.mjs beside the existing two rhubarb calls — `alignByRhubarb(pcm, { dialogText: hinglishText, duration: probed })`, guarded by the same `if (hinglishText)` already used for rules-hi at compare.mjs:95 — and add `['rhubarb+dialog', 'rhubarb+hinglish-dialog']` to the pairs array at compare.mjs:126. Everything it needs is already loaded in the loop. If that is declined, add one sentence to §4 saying this arm is not reproducible from the committed harness."},{"severity":"should-fix","file":"C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/lib/dictionary.mjs","issue":"THE MOST-QUOTED NUMBER IN THE PHASE COMES FROM AN UNTESTED PURE FUNCTION. Spec item 4: \"Unit tests for the parts that have a right answer.\" The four it names (JSON shape, duration scaling, sentence splitter, rule-table mapping) are all covered — 59 tests, 6 files, I re-ran them and they pass. But `coverage()` at dictionary.mjs:96 is a pure function with an exactly determinable right answer, and it produces the 48.2% Hinglish OOV figure that is §3's headline and the spine of the whole decision. It has no test. The tokeniser at dictionary.mjs:97 is `/[a-z']+/g` over a lowercased string, which silently drops every digit-bearing token and splits hyphenated words (the corpus's own \"iron-rich\" becomes two tokens) — both move the denominator of the headline rate. The `word(2)` alternative-pronunciation stripping in the parse loop (dictionary.mjs:79-83) is equally checkable and equally untested.","fix":"Add tools/lipsync/tests/dictionary.test.ts over a hand-built Set: tokenisation of hyphens, digits and apostrophes; oovRate arithmetic; empty input returning 0 rather than NaN; missingTypes deduplication; and the `(2)` suffix strip. No rhubarb install needed — coverage() takes the Set as an argument, so it tests in isolation."},{"severity":"improvement","file":"C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/lib/rules-driver.mjs","issue":"LITERAL SPEC WORDING NOT MET, THOUGH DISCLOSED. The brief specifies the fallback \"run per sentence over the KNOWN script and scaled to each sentence's measured duration\". rules-driver.mjs:33-35 computes weights as sums of mouth-movement units and calls `allocate(weights, durationSec)` — a share of the clip proportional to how much mouth movement a sentence contains, not how long it was spoken. Those are different quantities. This is disclosed honestly at scale.mjs:41 and in the builder's own gaps list, so it is not hidden. It matters because §7 promotes the rule table to the PRIMARY path for Hinglish, and this drift is that path's main known weakness — and the tool already shells out to ffmpeg (lib/audio.mjs), so a measured boundary is reachable.","fix":"Either derive real per-sentence boundaries with `ffmpeg -af silencedetect` and pass those spans to allocate() instead of weights, or add one line to the doc's §6 recording that the brief's \"each sentence's measured duration\" was approximated by weight share and why forced alignment was the only alternative."},{"severity":"note","file":"C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/lib/corpus.mjs","issue":"DEAD EXPORT WITH AN OVERCLAIMING DOCBLOCK. `allNarration()` at corpus.mjs:88 is exported and called nowhere — grep across every .mjs/.ts/.mts/.md in the phase's paths returns only its own definition. Its docblock states it serves \"the text-only arms (dictionary coverage, rule-table behaviour)\" and that \"this covers the whole tree rather than the one lesson that has been rendered\". Neither is true of anything that runs: compare.mjs computes dictionary coverage over the six class10-blood clips only. House rule 7 is that a comment must never overclaim; this one describes a whole-tree measurement that nothing performs.","fix":"Delete the function, or wire it into compare.mjs's dictionary section so the docblock becomes true — the all-lessons OOV baseline it describes would in fact strengthen §3, since it would show the English OOV rate across fifteen lessons rather than one."},{"severity":"note","file":"C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/corpus/hinglish.json","issue":"SPEC PREMISE WAS FALSE; DEVIATION IS CORRECT AND DISCLOSED. Item 3 asked for \"real Hinglish narration lines from the committed lessons (content/*/lesson.json)\". I checked all fifteen: every one carries `\"language\": \"en\"`. There is no Hinglish in the tree to pull, and tools/stt-spike/make-corpus.mjs had already recorded that same fact. The builder hand-authored the Hinglish and disclosed it in a twenty-line `_README` field inside the data file itself, in lib/corpus.mjs's docblock, and in the report. The \"pull from committed lessons\" half of the item was done (audioCorpus/allNarration read content/*/lesson.json). Recorded so this reads later as a spec premise that was wrong, not as quiet non-compliance.","fix":"No fix. If the phase brief is reused as a template, strike \"Hinglish\" from item 3 — the repo cannot satisfy it until a beat of Hinglish narration is rendered, which is the spend §7 already names as the next one."},{"severity":"note","file":"C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/lib/rules.mjs","issue":"NAMED DEPENDENCY SUBSTITUTED, WITH REASON GIVEN AT THE POINT OF DIVERGENCE. The brief specified \"TalkingHead's lipsync-en rule table (MIT, no weights, 12 KB)\". rules.mjs:5-13 credits that lineage but states the rules are \"WRITTEN rather than imported\" because lipsync-en targets 15 Oculus visemes while this repo commissions Rhubarb's 9, and mapping 15 down to 9 after the fact discards the chance to pick the right one of the nine. The per-shape weights at rules.mjs:44-54 are hand-set \"in the spirit of\" the upstream table and say so. The reasoning is sound and the provenance is honest. Flagged only so nobody later mistakes this for a vendored MIT file with upstream provenance to inherit.","fix":"No fix. Keep the lineage docblock intact if this file is ever refactored — it is the entire licensing story."}],"verdict":"PASS with fixes. Every major spec item was built and nothing was built outside the mandate.\n\nOWNERSHIP — CLEAN, verified not assumed. `git status --porcelain` shows only `tools/lipsync/` and `docs/research/lipsync-hinglish-result.md` from this phase; the other dirty paths are siblings'. Root `package.json`/`package-lock.json` are unmodified (`git diff --stat` empty) despite an npm install having happened — it went to `tools/lipsync/vendor/`, inside the owned path. `git check-ignore` confirms `.gitignore:1` covers `tools/lipsync/vendor/node_modules/`, and a dry-run add lists exactly 28 files, all inside ownership, with only the two small vendor manifests. `docs/research/README.md` and `avatar-3.md` are untouched — both correctly raised as handoffs instead.\n\nFORBIDDEN ACTIONS — NONE FOUND. No network or paid call anywhere in the owned code (grep for fetch/https/api/GEMINI/OPENAI/supabase across lib and bin returns nothing). No migration touched. No art, no sprite atlas, no renderer — all 28 files are .mjs/.d.mts/.ts/.json/.md, and avatar-3.md:239's step 2 (draw the mouths) is explicitly deferred to a human in §7, which is exactly what the brief demanded. I re-ran `npx vitest run tools/lipsync/tests` myself: 6 files, 59 tests, all pass — the builder's count is exact.\n\nONE THING THE BUILDER DID NOT CHECK, WHICH I DID, AND IT IS FINE. Root `tsconfig.json` includes `tools/**/*.ts`, so these new test files DO enter `npm run typecheck` for every concurrent phase. The builder's narrow verification omitted the root's `noUnusedLocals`/`noUnusedParameters`. I re-ran their files with those flags added: exit 0, clean. No cross-phase breakage. `--ignoreConfig` is also a real flag in this repo's TypeScript 6.0.3, and the three `avatar-3.md` line citations (191, 208, 239) are all accurate — unusually good for a repo where one claim in three has historically been false.\n\nTHE ONE REAL DROPPED ITEM is finding 1: the brief named exactly one required inclusion for the doc — the nine-shape list, \"because that list is what the illustrator gets commissioned against\" — and the doc recommends commissioning the art without containing the list. It is a nine-line paste from a file the same phase already wrote. Findings 2 and 3 are gaps in reproducibility and test coverage around the phase's two most load-bearing numbers. The remainder are disclosed deviations forced by a spec premise that the repo could not satisfy, and the builder handled those the right way: documented at the point of divergence rather than papered over."},"qualityI":{"phase":"I-lipsync-spike","lens":"QUALITY — tests real and RED-first, comments WHY-heavy and TRUE, every cost/contrast/timing claim recomputed, house patterns kept","findings":[{"severity":"blocker","file":"C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/lib/scale.mjs:8-13","issue":"The docblock justifies the `isLast` branch with arithmetic that does not hold, and quantifies a failure mode the code cannot have. It claims: \"three equal weights over one second is 0.333.. each. Rounded to the millisecond that is 0.999, so the mouth stops a millisecond early — once per sentence, roughly 120 sentences in a 12-minute lesson, an eighth of a second of accumulated lateness by the recap.\" VERIFIED FALSE BY MUTATION. I copied lib/ to a scratchpad, replaced line 31 with the plain proportional form (`const d = (duration * units[i].w) / total;`) and ran it: the three-equal-weights case still ends at exactly 1 — shortfall 0.000 ms, not 1 ms — because scaleUnits works in floats (sum error ~1e-16, not 1e-3) and normaliseCues rounds ABSOLUTE start/end times to milliseconds (format.mjs:56-57, 89-90), never per-cue durations. Running the mutant through alignByRules over all six class10-blood beats produced byte-identical timelines to the real module (JSON.stringify equal on all six; max end-time difference 0). The 'accumulated lateness' half cannot occur either: alignByRules re-anchors every sentence from allocate() and advances `cursor += spans[i]` (rules-driver.mjs:38-44), so a per-sentence shortfall would be filled with an idle cue, never carried forward. This repo's house rule names exactly this defect class — Wave 2 failed a phase for a docblock whose arithmetic did not hold — and the same invented failure is restated a second time at tools/lipsync/lib/scale.d.mts:4-6 (\"A mouth that finishes an eighth of a second before the voice does is the failure these two guarantees exist to prevent\"). The CODE is correct and worth keeping; only its stated reason is untrue.","fix":"Keep the `isLast` branch — exact summation by construction is a defensible property — but replace the invented arithmetic with what is measurable. Suggested: \"The proportional durations sum to `duration` only to float precision (~1e-16). Making the last cue take the remainder makes the sum exact by construction rather than by luck. MEASURED: removing this branch changes no cue on any class10-blood beat, because normaliseCues rounds absolute times rather than per-cue durations — so this is belt-and-braces against a future consumer that does not.\" Delete or restate the matching sentence in scale.d.mts:4-6."},{"severity":"should-fix","file":"C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/tests/rules-driver.test.ts:60-70","issue":"The test 'shifts the whole mouth earlier when asked to lead the audio' cannot fail, so it pins nothing. The first cue of a rules timeline starts at t=0, and normaliseCues clamps the lead-shifted negative start back to 0 (format.mjs:92), so `firstRealPlain.t` and `firstRealLed.t` are BOTH exactly 0. The assertion is therefore `0 < 0 + 0.0005`. I ran `alignByRules(NARRATION, 19.68, { leadMs: 0 })` through the same assertion: it passes — i.e. the test passes with the lever removed entirely. The lever does work (measured: plain[10].t = 1.658, led[10].t = 1.618, a clean 40 ms shift), but nothing in the suite would notice if it stopped. The comment above it says 'this only checks the lever works', which is the one thing it does not do.","fix":"Assert on a cue past the clamp, where the shift is observable: `const i = 10; expect(led[i].t).toBeCloseTo(plain[i].t - 0.04, 3);` (measured 1.658 -> 1.618). Keep the existing `validateTimeline(led)` check alongside it."},{"severity":"should-fix","file":"C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/tests/scale.test.ts:19-29","issue":"The test named 'sums to exactly the duration, with the last cue absorbing the rounding' asserts `toBeCloseTo(1, 6)`, which plain proportional division already satisfies (0.9999999999999999). It does not test the behaviour its name claims. Verified: with the `isLast` branch removed from scale.mjs, every assertion in this file still holds — 'lays cues end to end' (0.5/0.5), 'honours the start offset' (0.25), 'weights longer shapes longer', and this one. Combined with the finding above, the `isLast` branch is both unjustified in prose and unpinned by any test, which is how it survived review as a true-looking claim.","fix":"Assert exactness rather than closeness: `expect(end).toBe(1)`. Add a case where the difference is actually visible at the API boundary — e.g. `scaleUnits([{v:'A',w:1},{v:'B',w:1},{v:'C',w:1}], { start: 0, duration: 0.1 })` and assert the last cue's `t + d` is exactly 0.1 — or drop 'with the last cue absorbing the rounding' from the name so the test claims only what it checks."},{"severity":"improvement","file":"C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/docs/research/lipsync-hinglish-result.md:32","issue":"The throughput figure is the one measurement in the document that did not reproduce. §1 states '1.12-1.15x real time per pass ... 121.76 s of audio aligned in ~140 s', and §8 derives 'about 14 minutes of one laptop core' for a 12-minute lesson from it. On a clean re-run of bin/compare.mjs on this machine the +dialog pass took 125,763 ms for 121.76 s of audio (1.03x RT) and the bare pass 123,488 ms (1.01x RT); per clip the spread was 0.86x (recap) to 1.18x (wbc_platelets) — wider and lower-centred than the stated 1.12-1.15x band. The error is conservative (the doc overstates the cost, so nothing downstream breaks), but a range that narrow reads as a repeatable constant and is not one. Separately in §8: '497 KB of the .m4a narration' understates — the six files are 509,783 bytes = 497.8 KiB, so 498 KB; the derived 0.84 % is correct either way. The builder report's own figure of '508,915 bytes of audio measured on disk' is wrong by 868 bytes and does not appear in the artefact.","fix":"State it as measured and machine-dependent: e.g. 'about 1.0-1.2x real time, single-threaded, varying with machine load (measured 125.8 s of wall clock for 121.76 s of audio on the run behind this table)'. Round the audio figure to 498 KB, and correct 508,915 -> 509,783 in the phase report."},{"severity":"note","file":"C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/docs/research/lipsync-hinglish-result.md:188-197","issue":"Two precision problems in the defect section, both minor, neither false. (1) 'Its raw cues overlap each other and arrive out of order' — the overlap half is solidly measured and reproduced (168 violations in 794 raw cues, 21.2 %, exact), but the out-of-order half is much thinner than the phrasing suggests: in the rhubarb+dialog arm of hook.m4a — the arm the 168/794 figure comes from — I measured ZERO cues whose start precedes the previous cue's start (33 overlapping pairs, 0 out-of-order); the bare arm of the same clip has exactly 1 in 138. Out-of-order starts are also not separately counted in the 168, since validateTimeline reports them as overlaps. NOT VERIFIED across the other five clips. (2) The block labelled 'Verbatim, from hook.m4a' does reproduce exactly — but from the NO-SCRIPT pass (138 raw cues, which matches the builder's 138-cue smoke test), not the +dialog pass quoted in the sentence above it, whose cues at that instant are 0.49-0.76 E / 0.69-0.76 B. Same provenance question applies to the 'copied verbatim from a real run' comment at tools/lipsync/tests/format.test.ts:46-48, which is true but does not say which arm.","fix":"Reword to 'overlap each other — 168 violations in 794 raw cues — and, less often, start before their predecessor (1 cue in 138 in the no-script pass of hook.m4a)'. Label the quoted block '(no-script pass)' and add the same two words to the comment in format.test.ts:46-48."},{"severity":"note","file":"C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/docs/research/lipsync-hinglish-result.md:180-184","issue":"The claim that \"avatar-3.md's size estimate was right about the wire and wrong about the disk\" reads the source more charitably than the source reads. docs/research/avatar-3.md:208 says 'Roughly 2 KB per minute of narration, gzipped to nothing' — i.e. 2 KB/min is presented as the UNCOMPRESSED size, with gzip shrinking it further from there. Measured raw is 9.96-16.66 KB/min, so on its own terms the estimate was 5-8x low; that the gzipped figure lands at 2.06 KB/min is a coincidence, not a vindication of the estimate. 'The estimate stands where it counts' is the one sentence in this otherwise scrupulous document that is kinder to a prior claim than the evidence warrants.","fix":"Restate as: 'avatar-3.md:208 estimated roughly 2 KB/min and expected gzip to shrink it further. Measured, it is 9.96-16.66 KB/min raw — 5-8x the estimate — and 2.06-2.80 KB/min gzipped. The number to quote is the gzipped one, which happens to land where the estimate put the raw one.'"},{"severity":"note","file":"C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/bin/lipsync.mjs:123","issue":"`--lead-ms` is threaded into alignByRules only. On the rhubarb path (line 121) it is silently dropped, so `--driver rhubarb --lead-ms 40` accepts the flag, ignores it, and exits 0 with an unshifted timeline. The file's own header (lines 13-15) says 'It refuses rather than guessing — a build step that silently emits [the wrong thing] ... is invisible until a child is watching it', and USAGE line 32 documents the flag with no driver restriction. Silently accepting an inapplicable flag is off-pattern for this repo's reject-never-sanitise habit.","fix":"Either refuse before dispatch — `if (args.leadMs && driver === 'rhubarb') die('refused: --lead-ms is a rule-table lever; the rhubarb driver takes its timing from the audio.')` — or mark the flag '(rules driver only)' in USAGE line 32 and warn on stderr when it is dropped."},{"severity":"note","file":"C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/tests/rules-driver.test.ts:22-26","issue":"The comment calls the NARRATION constant 'The real opening beat of content/class10-blood, at its real measured length (19.68 s, content/class10-blood/audio/narration.json)'. The duration is right (narration.json gives hook durationMs 19680), but the text is the first two of the beat's four sentences — 159 of the beat's 258 characters (content/class10-blood/lesson.json). 62 % of the script is paired with 100 % of the clock and described as 'the real opening beat'. Harmless for these structural assertions, which do not depend on the ratio, but it is a comment asserting more than the constant holds.","fix":"Say what it is: 'the first two sentences of the opening beat of content/class10-blood, scaled to the whole beat's measured length (19.68 s, audio/narration.json) — the ratio does not matter to the structural assertions below.'"},{"severity":"note","file":"C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/lib/rhubarb-driver.mjs:14-18","issue":"The enumeration of the packed payload omits its largest item, and the omitted item is extra evidence for the phase's own conclusion. The comment describes the 37.0 MB rhubarb.data as 'the acoustic model and the 125,945-word English dictionary'; measured from the Emscripten manifest those are 6,609,647 B and 3,272,051 B, while /res/sphinx/en-us.lm.bin is 27,114,385 B — 73 % of the file, an English n-gram language model. docs/research/lipsync-hinglish-result.md:61-65 makes the same omission while arguing 'there is no phonetic recogniser in the build to select'. Nothing stated is false; the strongest single piece of evidence for the English-only argument is just missing from both lists.","fix":"Add it: the manifest's ten files are eight acoustic-model files (6.6 MB), cmudict-en-us.dict (3.3 MB) and en-us.lm.bin (27.1 MB, 73 % of the payload) — three English-only assets and no language-independent recogniser anywhere in the build."}],"verdict":"PASS, with one blocker-class comment to correct before this is built on. This is the best-evidenced phase I have reviewed in this tree: I independently re-ran the harness and every number in §3, §4, §5 and the fifth arm of docs/research/lipsync-hinglish-result.md reproduced EXACTLY — 731/741/1021/1142 cues, 6.00/6.09/8.39/9.38 cues-s, 21.2/21.8/28.0/27.1 % shut, 2.06/2.09/2.63/2.80 gz KB-min, the six disagreement figures with their per-clip ranges to one decimal, 168 violations in 794 raw cues, 125,945 dictionary words, 0.8 % / 48.2 % OOV with `litres` and `haemoglobin` as the two English misses, and 127-vs-119 cues at 15.3 % on the fifth arm. The headline finding is verified from the installed package rather than from documentation: dist/index.d.ts exposes `getLipSync(pcmData, options?: { dialogText?: string })` and RhubarbOptions has exactly one field, so avatar-3.md's `-r phonetic` mitigation genuinely is unreachable from the package avatar-3.md recommends. All five avatar-3.md line citations resolve, the vendor README phrase quoted in §2 is verbatim at its line 106, package sizes check to the byte (41,934,346 / 36,996,083), no binding.gyp or prebuilds exist so 'no native build' is true, and the sidecar precedent cited in format.d.mts is real (tools/prewarm/lib carries six). The refusal paths are reachable and were exercised: `--duration 0` and empty text both exit 1 rather than emitting a mouth. `npx vitest run tools/lipsync/tests` is 59 passing in 6 files, matching the report (the report's per-file count of 13 for format.test.ts is 12; 12+7+9+14+11+6 = 59). The test files typecheck clean under the repo's own tsconfig.base.json (strict, noUnusedLocals, allowImportingTsExtensions) via a scratchpad project naming only this phase's files — no whole-workspace typecheck was run. Scope was respected exactly: git status shows only tools/lipsync/ and the one research note, root package.json and package-lock.json untouched, vitest.config.ts already covered tools/*/tests so nothing foreign needed editing, and docs/research/README.md was correctly left alone and recorded as a handoff. British spelling and Rs throughout; no palette or UI surface to violate; no money spent and no migration pushed. WHAT MUST CHANGE: the scale.mjs docblock (repeated in scale.d.mts) justifies a real line of code with an invented failure mode, and I disproved it by mutation — removing the branch it defends produces byte-identical timelines on all six real beats and the exact case it names still lands at 0.000 ms of error. By this repo's own stated standard, with Wave 2's precedent named in the house rules, a docblock whose arithmetic does not hold is a phase failure, and the fix is two sentences of honest text over correct code. Two tests alongside it are vacuous in the same area: the lead-ms test passes with the lever set to zero, and the scale test named for exact summation asserts only closeness and survives the mutant. Fix those three and the phase is unambiguously sound. The honestGaps list is accurate and unusually complete — the acoustic-on-Hinglish gap really is the larger half, the 48.2 % really is a floor, and the rules-driver test really was written after its implementation and says so in its own docblock."},"criticI":{"phase":"I-lipsync-spike","lens":"ADVERSARIAL CRITIC","findings":[{"severity":"blocker","file":"C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/lib/rules.mjs:222 (also rules.mjs:2-3,28-32; bin/lipsync.mjs:13-15,130; README.md:104-106; docs/research/lipsync-hinglish-result.md:166-169)","issue":"THE FALLBACK IS SCRIPT-DEPENDENT, NOT LANGUAGE-INDEPENDENT, AND IT FAILS SILENTLY AS A CLOSED MOUTH. textToUnits tokenises with /[A-Za-zÀ-ɏ']+|[^A-Za-zÀ-ɏ']+/ and wordToShapes strips /[^a-z]/, so Devanagari falls into the 'everything else' branch and becomes an X (idle) pause. REPRODUCED through the shipped CLI:\n  $ node tools/lipsync/bin/lipsync.mjs --text dev.txt --duration 19.68 --driver rules -o dev.json\n  dev.json  driver=rules  1 cues  19.68s  27 bytes\n  EXIT=0\n  [{\"t\":0,\"d\":19.68,\"v\":\"X\"}]\nA full beat of Hindi narration produces a mouth that is shut for 19.68 seconds, exit 0, no warning. validateTimeline() returns [] for it. The only guard at bin/lipsync.mjs:130 is cues.length===0, and this timeline has one cue, so the guard the CLI is proudest of is defeated by the exact input it names. Mixed script is worse than mute: 'Dekho — यह चार अलग टीम है, plasma सबसे बड़ा है।' over 12s yields 12 cues with a 1000 ms mean hold (measured 107-167 ms elsewhere) — two Latin words stretched across eleven.\nTHREE PLACES OVERCLAIM THE OPPOSITE, which is the house-rule-7 failure mode this repo has shipped before: bin/lipsync.mjs:13-15 'It refuses rather than guessing — a build step that silently emits an empty timeline ships a tutor whose mouth never moves'; README.md:104-106 repeats it verbatim; rules.mjs:2-3 'immune to language ... This file is that immunity'; and the research doc §5 states 'It is a letter table, so romanised Hindi is simply input. Nothing degrades because there is nothing language-specific to degrade.' The qualifier 'romanised' is doing load-bearing work that the surrounding claims then drop. This matters precisely because §7.1 of the same document promotes this driver to PRIMARY for Hinglish — the one language in this product whose native script is not Latin.","fix":"(1) In alignByRules (lib/rules-driver.mjs:165), after building perSentence, refuse when the text has content but produced no moving shape: if every unit is 'X', or if the fraction of non-whitespace characters that contributed a shape is below a floor, return [] — the refusal the contract already promises. (2) In bin/lipsync.mjs, add a guard beside line 130: refuse a timeline whose open-mouth time is zero ('refused: this text produced a mouth that never opens — the rule table reads Latin letters only'). (3) Correct the three comments and the doc line to say what is true: the table is immune to LANGUAGE and dependent on SCRIPT; romanised Hinglish is input, Devanagari is not. (4) Add the two tests in finding 7."},{"severity":"should-fix","file":"C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/docs/research/lipsync-hinglish-result.md:249-258 (§7.1, against its own §5:161-165 and §5:174-179)","issue":"THE RECOMMENDED REMEDY IS TWELVE TIMES LARGER THAN THE DEFECT IT TREATS, BY THE DOCUMENT'S OWN TABLES. §3 measures 48.2% Hinglish OOV. §5 then bounds what OOV can cost: removing the script entirely from English audio moves the mouth 15.2% shape / 3.0% open-shut, because 'the dominant input is the acoustics, not the dictionary'. So the dictionary channel — the ONLY channel §3 measures — is worth at most ~3.0% on the coarse question. Swapping to the rule table costs 35.8% open/shut (§5:176). §7.1 therefore recommends a change that perturbs the mouth roughly twelve times more than the failure it is meant to avoid, on the strength of a dictionary number the same document has just shown to be nearly inert. The fifth arm (§4:136-146) points the same way: a Hinglish script over English audio 'does not poison the recogniser; it simply stops helping' — which is an argument for KEEPING Rhubarb on Hinglish audio, not replacing it. And no arm anywhere measured the rule table against Hinglish audio, because none exists — so §7.1 is a default chosen with zero evidence bearing on it.","fix":"Rewrite §7.1 from a default switch into a blocked decision: 'The recogniser for Hinglish is UNDECIDED, blocked on one measurement (§7.4). Provisionally Rhubarb stays primary on any rendered narration, Hinglish included, because arm 5 shows it degrades by ignoring the script rather than by misusing it, and because §5 bounds that loss at 3.0% open/shut while switching drivers costs 35.8%.' Keep the true part of §7.1 — that -r phonetic is unreachable — as a finding about avatar-3.md, not as grounds for a driver change."},{"severity":"should-fix","file":"C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/docs/research/lipsync-hinglish-result.md:213-219 (§6, first NOT MEASURED) and the builder report's honestGaps[0]","issue":"THE ONE MEASUREMENT THAT WOULD CLOSE THE PHASE'S OWN QUESTION WAS DEFERRED AS EXPENSIVE AND COSTS ABOUT ONE RUPEE. Both the doc and the report justify the headline gap with 'Rendering Hinglish narration means a real Gemini TTS call, which this phase did not make' — with no price attached. The repo prices it itself: tools/record-class/src/cost.ts:11 AUDIO_TOKENS_PER_SECOND = 32 and :14 USD_PER_MILLION_AUDIO_TOKENS = 20. One 19.68 s beat = 630 tokens = $0.0126 ≈ Rs 1.1. All six beats, 121.76 s = 3,896 tokens = $0.078 ≈ Rs 6.9 (computed with those two constants). Not spending unilaterally was correct under house rule 5. Leaving the number out was not: an unpriced blocker reads as expensive and gets deferred forever, and this one is a rounding error against a product built to a Rs 5/student-hour ceiling — the doc's own §8 argues that ceiling at length while the experiment that would settle its chartered question sits unpriced two sections earlier.","fix":"Add one line to §6's first bullet and to §7: 'Closing this costs Rs 1.1 for one beat or Rs 6.9 for all six (32 audio tokens/s at $20/M, tools/record-class/src/cost.ts:11,14). It needs a human yes, not a budget.' Name the renderer (tools/record-class) and the beat to render (hook, 19.68 s, the corpus line already written at tools/lipsync/corpus/hinglish.json)."},{"severity":"should-fix","file":"C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/docs/research/lipsync-hinglish-result.md:264-281 (§7, 'What a human must now do')","issue":"THE PRESCRIBED HUMAN STEP CANNOT ANSWER THE QUESTION THE PHASE WAS CHARTERED ON. It asks a person to play nine placeholder mouths against content/class10-blood/audio and watch both drivers side by side. That audio is English — the document says so three times (§1:34-40, corpus.mjs:130-135, README.md:138-142). An afternoon spent on it settles an aesthetic question about English and returns nothing about Hinglish, which is the entire brief. The doc then sequences the Hinglish render AFTER the watch ('If it does not survive, the next spend is Hinglish audio'), so the cheap, decisive step is queued behind the expensive, indecisive one.","fix":"Swap the order in §7 and say why: render one Hinglish beat first (Rs 1.1, finding 3), then hold the single human watch against HINGLISH audio with both drivers. One afternoon of attention then answers the chartered question instead of a neighbouring one, and the 35.8% open/shut divergence gets adjudicated on the language that matters."},{"severity":"should-fix","file":"C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/docs/research/lipsync-hinglish-result.md:286-296 (§8) and :251 (§7.1), against C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/README.md:143-146","issue":"THE BUILD/RUNTIME JOINT IS UNSTATED, AND IT IS WHERE HINGLISH ACTUALLY LIVES — a joint with phase G. §8's cost claim ('Alignment is a build step on a laptop ... Runtime is reading an array. No analyser, no FFT, no per-frame CPU') is true only for the six pre-rendered .m4a beats. Every Hinglish utterance this product speaks is runtime browser speech: packages/player/src/narration.ts:33,71 createBrowserTts over window.speechSynthesis, reached through speakSafely at apps/classroom/src/screens/ClassRoom.tsx:496, and phase G's whyProbe spec routes the authored `why` down exactly that path. There is no clip and no measured duration there — only estimateSpeechMs(text, 140 wpm) at packages/player/src/narration.ts:23. So §7.1's 'the rule table becomes the primary for Hinglish narration' names a production path that does not exist: README.md:145 says in its own words that Hinglish 'is never stored and cannot be pulled out of the tree'. If the rule table is to drive Hinglish, it runs IN THE BROWSER, per utterance, on a cheap Android phone, against an ESTIMATED duration — which compounds the movement-proportional allocation drift the doc already names at §6:228-233, and which §6 lists as 'NOT MEASURED: any device cost' without connecting the two.","fix":"State the split in §7 and in README: Rhubarb = build step over committed narration audio (English today); rule table = the only driver that can run at runtime, in-browser, over generated speech, against estimateSpeechMs rather than a measured clock. Then scope §8's cost claim to the build path explicitly, and add to §6 the two runtime gaps that follow — per-utterance rule-table cost on a low-end phone, and drift when the clock is an estimate instead of a measurement."},{"severity":"improvement","file":"C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/docs/research/lipsync-hinglish-result.md:136-146 (§4, fifth arm)","issue":"'STATISTICALLY INDISTINGUISHABLE' IS AN UNEARNED CLAIM IN A DOCUMENT THAT REFUSES ONE ON THE NEXT PAGE. It compares two point measurements of a deterministic aligner on a single clip (15.3% vs 12.4%) with no repeated runs and no variance estimate anywhere in the harness — there is nothing for 'statistically' to attach to. Worse, the direction runs against the framing: the wrong-language script moved the output FURTHER from the correct-script run (15.3%) than no script at all did (12.4%), which is mild mis-biasing, not the clean evaporation the sentence 'it simply stops helping' describes. The doc spends §5:152-157 and metrics.mjs:1-22 refusing an accuracy number on exactly this discipline, then relaxes it for the one number that supports the story.","fix":"Replace with the measurement: 'On one clip, a Hinglish script moved the result 15.3% from the correct-script run, against 12.4% for no script at all — slightly worse than no script, not merely neutral. One clip, one deterministic run, no variance estimate: too small a margin to call it poisoning and too small a sample to call it anything else.' Drop 'statistically indistinguishable' here and in the phase summary."},{"severity":"improvement","file":"C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/tests/rules-driver.test.ts:36 and C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/tests/rules.test.ts:104","issue":"THE TWO HINGLISH TESTS ASSERT NOTHING A MUTE MOUTH WOULD FAIL. rules-driver.test.ts:36 asserts only validateTimeline(alignByRules(HINGLISH, 19.68)).toEqual([]); rules.test.ts:104 asserts units.length > 10 and that every shape is one of the nine. The Devanagari output from finding 1 — [{t:0,d:19.68,v:'X'}] — satisfies the first outright, and a text of pure pauses satisfies the spirit of the second. Across all 59 tests there is no assertion anywhere that the mouth ever opens. That is the single missing assertion that would have caught the blocker, in a suite otherwise built test-first.","fix":"Add to rules-driver.test.ts: (a) for the existing HINGLISH fixture, assert open-mouth time is a substantial share of the clip (sum of d over cues whose v is not in SHUT_SHAPES > 0.3 * duration) and that cue count is well above 10; (b) a new case asserting alignByRules('<one Devanagari sentence>', 19.68) refuses — returns [] — once finding 1 is fixed."},{"severity":"improvement","file":"C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/tools/lipsync/lib/format.mjs:106-112","issue":"LATENT: normaliseCues DISCARDS AN ENVELOPING CUE'S TAIL AND REPLACES IT WITH A SHUT MOUTH. The clipping pass compares each cue only with the IMMEDIATELY next one, so a cue spanning two or more later cues is truncated to the first of them and the remainder is filled with idle. Measured: normaliseCues([{0.00-1.00 D},{0.10-0.20 B},{0.30-0.40 A}], {duration:1}) returns D only for 0-0.1 s and turns 0.4-1.0 s — 60% of the open cue — into X, with validateTimeline reporting zero problems. Same silent-substitution failure class as finding 1. HONEST BOUND, measured rather than assumed: I ran the real aligner over content/class10-blood/audio/components.m4a and counted 98 raw cues, 18 pairwise overlaps, and ZERO enveloping cues. So this is a latent robustness gap in the boundary that the renderer handoff calls mandatory, not an active defect in today's output — and it would bite a different aligner version or a different aligner.","fix":"Track a running max end across the clipped pass (or clip against the max start of all remaining cues) so an enveloping cue keeps its span after the nested cues end, rather than collapsing to idle. Add a unit test with the three-cue fixture above. If the behaviour is deliberate, say so in the docblock with the measurement — 0 occurrences in 98 real cues — rather than leaving it silent."},{"severity":"note","file":"C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/docs/research/lipsync-hinglish-result.md:190 and the builder report's handoffs[4]","issue":"'168 OF ITS 794 RAW CUES (21.2%) VIOLATE THE TIMELINE CONTRACT' COUNTS PROBLEMS, NOT CUES. validateTimeline (format.mjs:145-157) pushes one string per failing CHECK, so a single cue that both overlaps its predecessor and has a non-positive duration contributes two entries. The doc's §5 wording — '168 format violations in 794 raw cues' — is exactly right; the builder report and the renderer handoff restate it as '168 of its 794 raw cues', a different and unmeasured claim. The conclusion (normaliseCues is mandatory) is unaffected, and the 18 overlaps I counted independently on one clip support it.","fix":"Use the doc's wording everywhere, or count distinct offending cues (new Set of indices) in compare.mjs and report that instead."},{"severity":"note","file":"C:/Users/Ashish Sharma/curie/docs/research/lipsync-hinglish-result.md:294 (§8) and the builder report's costEffect","issue":"THE AUDIO BYTE TOTAL IS WRONG BY 868 BYTES. The report states '508,915 bytes of audio measured on disk'. The six .m4a files sum to 509,783 (65,072 + 81,615 + 90,059 + 89,042 + 106,114 + 77,881). The 868-byte difference is exactly the size of narration.json, so the manifest appears to have been substituted for one clip in the sum. The doc's rounded '497 KB' survives either way (509,783/1024 = 497.8) and the 0.84% ratio is unchanged to two figures — but in a report that says 'measured on disk', the measurement should be the one on disk.","fix":"Correct the figure to 509,783 bytes in §8 and in the cost arithmetic. No conclusion moves."}],"verdict":"STRONG PHASE WITH ONE REAL HOLE AND ONE DECISION THAT ITS OWN NUMBERS CONTRADICT. I tried to break the thing the lens most feared — a harness quoting an accuracy it cannot know — and could not: metrics.mjs:1-22, compare.mjs:8-10 and the harness's closing paragraph all refuse it explicitly, and there is no `accuracy` field on any type. That refusal is the best thing here and it held up under attack.\n\nWHAT I REPRODUCED WITH REAL COMMANDS (four independent checks, no git writes, no spend):\n- `npx vitest run tools/lipsync/tests` — 6 files, 59 tests, green in 5.06 s. The builder's headline test claim is true.\n- The dictionary measurement re-run end to end: 125,945 words / 134,723 entries; EN 252 tokens / 0.8% OOV (misses exactly `litres`, `haemoglobin`); HI 278 tokens / 48.2% OOV; `beta` present, `hai` and `dekho` absent. Every number in §3, including the two British-spelling misses and the `beta` trap, reproduces exactly. This is the hardest claim in the phase and it survives.\n- `npx tsc` over the six test files WITH the repo's own `noUnusedLocals`/`noUnusedParameters` (which the builder's ad-hoc invocation omitted): clean. The stitch typecheck risk I suspected is not there.\n- The real aligner over `components.m4a`: 98 raw cues, 18 pairwise overlaps, 0 enveloping — which downgraded my finding 8 from a bug to a latent gap, and confirmed the stdout/stderr split is clean so piped JSON is not corrupted by rhubarb's DEBUG lines.\n\nTHE ONE BLOCKER: the fallback that §7 has just promoted to primary for Hinglish emits a 19.68-second closed mouth, exit 0, for Devanagari narration — reproduced through the shipped CLI, past `validateTimeline`, past the `cues.length === 0` guard, while three comments and the README claim it refuses exactly this. The table is script-dependent, not language-independent, and nowhere says so.\n\nTHE ONE DECISION TO REVERSE: §7.1 makes the rule table primary for Hinglish. By the document's own §5, the dictionary damage it treats is bounded at 3.0% open/shut, while the swap itself costs 35.8% — a remedy twelve times larger than the defect, chosen with zero measurement on any Hinglish audio. Pair that with the fact that the decisive experiment costs about Rs 1.1 by this repo's own cost model and was deferred unpriced, and that the prescribed human watch uses ENGLISH audio and cannot answer the chartered question at all. Three cheap changes fix all of it: refuse the mute mouth, re-word §7.1 as blocked-not-decided, and put the Rs 1.1 Hinglish render in front of the human watch instead of behind it.\n\nThe honesty discipline in this phase is genuinely above the repo's average — the gaps list is long, specific and mostly self-incriminating, and the one place it slips ('statistically indistinguishable') is the one place the story benefits."}}
const CARRIED_FIX = { 'G-classroom': CARRY.fixG, 'H-contest-door': CARRY.fixH }

log('Wave 3 finish: fix:I never ran, and no phase was ever verified. Carrying six finished reports in.')

phase('Fix')

const results = await pipeline(
  PHASES,
  async (p) => {
    const carried = CARRIED_FIX[p.key]
    if (carried) {
      log('fix:' + p.key + ' replayed from the interrupted run (' + (carried.applied || []).length + ' findings applied).')
      return carried
    }
    return await agent(fixPrompt(p, CARRY.buildI, [CARRY.scopeI, CARRY.qualityI, CARRY.criticI].filter(Boolean)),
      { label: 'fix:' + p.key, phase: 'Fix', schema: FIX_SCHEMA, effort: 'high' })
  },
  (fix, p) =>
    agent(verifyPrompt(p, fix), { label: 'verify:' + p.key, phase: 'Verify', schema: VERIFY_SCHEMA, effort: 'high' })
      .then((verify) => ({ phase: p.key, title: p.title, fix, verify })),
)

const done = results.filter(Boolean)
log(done.length + '/3 phases verified.')

return {
  phases: done.map((d) => ({
    phase: d.phase,
    title: d.title,
    ready: d.verify?.ready ?? false,
    claimsRechecked: d.verify?.claimsRechecked ?? 0,
    overturned: d.verify?.overturned ?? [],
    failedChecks: (d.verify?.checks ?? []).filter((c) => !c.pass),
    fixed: d.fix?.applied?.length ?? 0,
    skipped: d.fix?.skipped ?? [],
    verifySummary: d.verify?.summary ?? '',
  })),
  detail: done,
}
